course: dictionary
lesson: 2010
title: Condense: from vapour to drops, or shorter
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To condense means for a gas to cool and turn into a liquid, like steam turning into water drops. It can also mean to make something thicker by taking out water, or to make writing shorter while keeping the main ideas.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. «Air cools as it rises, so water vapour condenses high up into clouds.» Air that cools is getting colder, and vapour is a gas, so condenses must mean the gas is turning into tiny drops of liquid.
explain: Try the swap check. Put your meaning in place of the word: «Water vapour turns into liquid drops high up into clouds.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: To condense means to change from a gas into a liquid when it cools.
fact: Steam condensing on a cold window makes tiny drops of water.
fact: «Condensed milk» is milk with water removed, so it is thick and sweet.
fact: «Condense your notes» means make them shorter but keep the main ideas.
fact: Condensation is why a cold can gets wet on the outside, and it is not a leak.
exercise: «Air cools as it rises, so water vapour condenses high up into clouds.» What does condenses mean here? | Turns from a liquid into a gas | *Turns from a gas into tiny liquid drops | Melts and disappears | Freezes into solid ice
exercise: «A cold can gets wet on the outside because water vapour from the air condenses on it; it is not leaking.» Why is the can wet? | It was out in the rain | Someone poured water on it | *Water vapour from the air turns into drops on the cold can | The can is leaking inside
exercise: Your teacher says, «Condense your notes into five short lines.» What does condense mean? | Make the notes bigger with more detail | *Make them shorter but keep the main ideas | Pour the notes into a cup | Freeze the notes
test: Which change is condensing? | Liquid to gas | *Gas to liquid | Solid to liquid | Liquid to solid
test: What is condensed milk? | Milk poured into a cup | Milk that has turned into steam | Milk that has been frozen solid | *Milk with water removed, so it is thick
exam: Which best explains why a bathroom mirror steams up? | The mirror is leaking water from behind | The glass is melting in the heat | Someone breathed on it on purpose | *Hot water vapour from the bath condenses into tiny drops on the cool mirror
